WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.136
Before anything else, picture this.

00:00:02.536 --> 00:00:10.791
Sudoku, permutations and seating plans all ask you to try a choice, see if it works, and undo it if not. That is backtracking.

00:00:11.991 --> 00:00:12.920
Pause and think.

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:16.954
What makes backtracking faster than trying every full combination?

00:00:17.354 --> 00:00:24.668
Hold your answer; we will check it at the end. And whenever a new word comes up, we stop for a moment and explain it.

00:00:25.868 --> 00:00:31.174
This lesson covers backtracking: trying choices, and undoing them when they do not work.

00:00:31.201 --> 00:00:41.011
New word: backtracking. Try a choice, explore, undo it if it fails, try the next. Picture it like this: Walking a maze and stepping back at dead ends.

00:00:43.551 --> 00:00:46.407
Picture walking through a maze with a ball of string.

00:00:46.857 --> 00:00:50.235
At each fork you pick a path and unroll string behind you.

00:00:50.685 --> 00:00:55.620
If you hit a dead end, you walk back along the string to the last fork and try the next path.

00:00:56.070 --> 00:00:59.634
Rolling the string back up is the undo step. That is backtracking.

00:01:00.984 --> 00:01:04.896
Here is permutations of one, two, three as a call stack.

00:01:04.951 --> 00:01:14.018
New word: stack. Last-in-first-out structure; also the memory area holding function calls. Picture it like this: A stack of plates.

00:01:15.668 --> 00:01:22.158
The first call chooses one. It calls deeper, which chooses two, then three. A full row is recorded.

00:01:22.608 --> 00:01:27.798
Then the deepest frame returns, and two is un-chosen so three can be tried in its place.

00:01:28.248 --> 00:01:33.380
Each frame on the stack is one decision. The depth of the stack is the length of the path.

00:01:34.730 --> 00:01:37.342
The template is always the same three lines.

00:01:37.792 --> 00:01:41.426
Choose: add an option to the current path and mark it used.

00:01:41.876 --> 00:01:44.743
Explore: recurse to make the next decision.

00:01:45.193 --> 00:01:49.652
Un-choose: remove it again, so the path is clean for the next option.

00:01:50.102 --> 00:01:54.397
And when the path is complete, save a COPY of it, not the path itself.

00:01:55.747 --> 00:01:58.325
Pruning is what makes backtracking fast enough.

00:01:58.775 --> 00:02:02.304
Pruning means refusing to go down a path that cannot succeed.

00:02:02.754 --> 00:02:09.407
N Queens prunes any square already attacked. Word Search Two prunes any letters that are not a prefix of a word.

00:02:09.857 --> 00:02:15.139
Good pruning can turn hours into milliseconds, even though the worst case stays exponential.

00:02:16.489 --> 00:02:17.569
Interview tips.

00:02:18.019 --> 00:02:21.281
Draw the decision tree for a tiny input before coding.

00:02:21.731 --> 00:02:27.525
State the complexity honestly: subsets is two to the n, permutations is n factorial.

00:02:27.975 --> 00:02:32.770
For duplicates in the input, sort first and skip equal neighbors at the same depth.

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:40.273
Choose, explore, un-choose, and prune early. Try Subsets, Permutations and N Queens.

00:02:40.252 --> 00:02:41.506
Back to the question.

00:02:41.906 --> 00:02:45.540
What makes backtracking faster than trying every full combination?

00:02:45.940 --> 00:02:49.806
Pruning: you stop exploring a branch as soon as it breaks a rule.

00:02:51.006 --> 00:02:51.877
Quick recap.

00:02:52.277 --> 00:02:54.285
Choose, explore, un-choose.

00:02:54.685 --> 00:02:55.475
Prune early.

00:02:55.875 --> 00:02:57.547
Build the answer in a path.
